==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|cōdex}}, variant form of {{m|la|caudex||tree trunk, book, notebook}}; compare {{m|en|caudex}} (in botany). {{doublet|en|code}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|kōʹdĕks}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkəʊdɛks/|ref1={{R:OED Online|title=codex|code=50043046}}|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkoʊdɛks/|a=GenAm}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-codex.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyphenation|en|co|dex}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛks|s=2}}
* {{homophones|en|codecs}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|codices|+}}

# An [[early]] [[manuscript]] [[book]].
# A [[book]] bound in the modern manner, by joining pages, as opposed to a rolled [[scroll]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Margalit Fox|title=Look It Up? Only if You’re Dishonest and Ignorant|work=The New York Times|date=2022-02-15|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/15/books/review/index-a-history-of-the-dennis-duncan.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=From its inception, the index has provided a window onto the history of the book, for it took the advent of a particular type of book — the '''codex''', a sheaf of pages fastened along one edge — to make an index a practical possibility.}}
# An [[official]] [[list]] of [[medicine]]s and [[medicinal]] [[ingredient]]s.

